{
	title: 'Model and effort read as plain words; short names when tight',
	spec: `The status row names the model and its effort as plain words: '<name>' at the model's default effort, '<name> <level>' otherwise, e.g. 'Opus 5.5' and 'Opus 5.5 high'. No parentheses, separator dot, 'default' or 'default/unknown'.

<name> is the official display name (task qq) when the row fits, else a short name, in the terminal and on the web alike (so phones get the short name). Short names exist only for Claude and GPT, the most used families: Claude drops 'Claude' ('Claude Opus 5.5' → 'Opus 5.5'); GPT with a tier puts the tier first ('GPT-6.1 Sol' → 'Sol 6.1', 'GPT-6 Luna' → 'Luna 6', 'GPT-5.3 Codex' → 'Codex 5.3'); plain 'GPT-5.5' stays. Other names are already short and stay. One function in src/common derives both.

The model picker (task pv) shows each label once, without stacked parentheses: category headers name the model Enter picks by its ID without the provider, as in the old Hal: 'openai (default: gpt-6.1-sol)'; categories without a default show none. Picker text never puts two spaces in a row as a separator; only indentation and column alignment pad. The default model row also carries a mark. The picker holds one effort draft, starting at the session's effort: only the selected row shows it, as the level it gives that model (snapped to the nearest), in ‹ › — a bare level, or 'default (<level>)', the only place the default's level is named; ←/→ change the draft. Hints follow the row: on a model '←/→: lower/higher effort, enter: pick, esc: cancel' (no ←/→ without effort control); on a category '←/→: close/open, enter: pick default|open, esc: cancel'. The picker title shows the selection as a model string, e.g. 'Model: anthropic/claude-opus-5-5:high'. effort.label in src/common/effort.ts no longer returns 'default/unknown' or nested parentheses.

Block headers (tasks hp, vwh) always use the short name, whatever the width, since they repeat on every block: model text reads 'Hal (<short name>)' or 'Hal (<short name> <level>)', e.g. 'Hal (Opus 5.5 high)', with the effort the block recorded; thinking reads 'Thinking' alone, its reply header naming model and effort. The model repeats on every header because users switch models often; a plugin may override the title function.

Tests: name shortening for Claude, tiered GPT, plain GPT and another family; status text at default and non-default effort.

Selection changes by Up/Down refresh the hint, title and effort marker immediately, not only after typing or changing effort. The marker appears only on the highlighted model row. A singleton family such as Haiku shows its short catalog name once ('Haiku 4.5'), never the dated raw ID beside the name; its complete ID remains in the selection title and command. In grouped families, the short model-name postfix is on the left (6-astra, 6.1-sol, 5.5), matching the hierarchy; effort remains on the highlighted row. The official name and complete ID are in the existing right-aligned detail column, e.g. GPT-6 Astra · openai/gpt-6-astra. Keep the current layout, category defaults, and selection-sensitive hints; this changes column ordering only. Singleton Haiku shows Haiku 4.5 only. Names remain readable by wrapping on the web. Rows without a useful distinct tree label show the name once. These rules apply to terminal and web. Regression tests navigate actual category, effort-capable and no-effort rows and confirm the visible default matches Enter's command.`,
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		"Clients learn each model's default effort from the model-names event (defaults map, built by models.nameEvent), so headers can leave the default unsaid; the status row's Stats.effort is set by the host only when it differs from the default. The web status row renders both names and CSS swaps them below 80ch; the terminal swaps when left plus all right groups overflow.",
		"First version kept an effort per row, so unselected rows showed overrides (e.g. 'Claude Opus 5 max'): too large a configuration space. Replaced by one draft (Tree.effort) mapped per row with effort.closest, shared with host snapping.",
		"Restored invariant: selection metadata (help, title, and sole effort marker) always describes the highlighted row. modals.step changed selected on Up/Down, but picker.step previously returned before refreshing presentation. picker.selection now updates only the old/new selected rows, without rebuilding or sorting the catalog. Haiku's dated raw ID leaked into its row because a singleton family deliberately has no family category; it now uses the short catalog name once. Category headers explicitly name Enter's default; grouped catalog names use the existing values column, right-aligned by each client. One-off terminal checks at 52/96 columns and real headless browser checks at 390/1280 pixels exercised Up/Down, leaf/category hints, singleton Haiku, wrapped full names, input focus, 44px phone rows and no horizontal overflow; no Solid diagnostics.",
		"Corrected hierarchy after user feedback: human-readable names are left/primary, complete machine IDs right/secondary. The previous implementation put ID-derived version labels left and catalog names right, contrary to the user's requested priority."
	],
	needs: ['w7', 'pv', 'qq'],
	foldInto: ['pv', 'w7']
}
